{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Yes. Under the Manual, a general letter consenting to future applications must identify the applicant receiving the consent, although it need not identify that applicant’s trade mark or specific goods and services.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5",
          "quote": "A general letter of consent confirming that one party is prepared to consent to any trade mark applications made by another party may be considered."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5",
          "quote": "It must also identify the applicant to whom the general consent is being given. However, the general consent document need not identify the applicant’s trade mark or the specific goods and services being claimed."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual says the general letter must meet the same requirements as a specific letter concerning the consenting owner’s name and the signatory, and should not contain conditions.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part27/4/4/2/5",
          "quote": "The general letter of consent must meet the same requirements as a specific letter of consent in relation to the name of the owner providing the consent and to the signatory of the letter (detailed above) and should not contain any conditions (see 3.2.1)."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For those name and signatory requirements, the Manual requires the consenting owner’s name to match the database and the signatory to be the owner of the conflicting trade mark or someone authorised to sign on that owner’s behalf.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part27/4/4/2/3",
          "quote": "the name of the owner supplying “consent” corresponds to the name on the data base. The letter may only be taken into account if the names are the same; the signatory to the letter is the owner of the conflicting trade mark or a person authorised to sign on behalf of that person;"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For formality issues, the Manual directs that the applicant be invited to comply in the most expeditious way available.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "The applicant should be invited to comply with formality requirements in the most expeditious way available."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Where only one or two minor formality matters need resolving, the Manual directs an attempt to contact the applicant by telephone or email, requesting the information through online services and recording the contact on file.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "8.1 Where there are only one or two minor formalities matters that have to be resolved an attempt should be made to contact the applicant by telephone or email requesting that they provide the relevant information through the online services page. Appropriate records should be attached to the file, either a Record of Conversation or a copy of the email that was sent."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If the requested information has not arrived through online services one week after contact, the examiner should issue a report about it if necessary.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "8.2 Where a request has been made by telephone or email but no information has been received through the online services page by one week after the contact was made the examiner should issue a report regarding the requested information if this is necessary."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If other matters already require a report, the formality requirements should be included in that report.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "8.3 If other matters have to be dealt with in any case then the formality requirements should be included in the report to be issued."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Email can be used for discussion and clarification, but any request or consent resulting in amendment of the application must be submitted through online services before it can be actioned.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "Please note – subsequent discussion with, and queries from, an applicant can still be handled via email. However any response which will result in the application being amended must be submitted through the online services page."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "If they request or consent to amendments, they should be advised that this must be submitted via the online services page before it can be actioned."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual states that unmet formality requirements require rejection under section 33(3).",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part10/8#1",
          "quote": "Where formality requirements are not met, the Registrar must reject the application as not being in accordance with this Act (section 33(3))."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied legislative passage contains only the opening of section 33(3), so it is insufficient to independently explain the statutory conditions for rejection; the supplied material may not cover every relevant requirement or step.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s33(3)",
          "quote": "(3) If the Registrar is satisfied that:"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
  "concepts_used": [
    "GC-0029",
    "GC-0116",
    "GC-0084",
    "GC-0044",
    "GC-0124",
    "GC-0179",
    "GC-0006"
  ]
}
